WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:05.420
The Fast Saga might be coming to a close - but
the next flick in the main series still might

00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:06.520
not be the last.

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:11.129
In keeping with the franchise's "more is more"
aesthetic, star Vin Diesel has suggested that

00:00:11.129 --> 00:00:13.799
Fast 10 might be split into two parts.

00:00:13.799 --> 00:00:17.980
He made his remarks in a recent, wide-ranging
interview with Total Film magazine.

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:22.180
The bombshell about the final Fast flick came
as Diesel was discussing possibilities for

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:26.210
the future of the franchise, which he hopes
will extend long beyond the life of the main

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:28.029
series in the form of spin-offs.

00:00:28.029 --> 00:00:32.880
Of course, the first of these - Fast and Furious
Presents: Hobbs &amp; Shaw - hit theaters last

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:36.590
summer, pleasing the heck out of audiences
and cleaning up at the box office to the tune

00:00:36.590 --> 00:00:38.840
of $760 million worldwide.

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:41.450
No doubt, fans were shouting, "Make more!"

00:00:41.450 --> 00:00:43.870
"No one tells me what to do!"

00:00:43.870 --> 00:00:45.640
Anyway, Diesel said:

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:51.260
"The universe is so robust and so rich with
talent and rich with story that, on one level,

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:55.030
it's totally feasible to have spinoffs, and
I think that's something that is inevitable.

00:00:55.030 --> 00:00:58.890
[Studio] Universal deserves it because of
how much they've invested in this little saga,

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:01.260
and it'd be good to give back to Universal.

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:05.780
And for the fans, should Fast 10 parts one
and two be the conclusion, it would be nice

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:08.430
for this world to continue for generations
to come."

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:13.080
Generally speaking, two-part conclusions are
more commonly associated with film adaptations

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:17.640
of popular books - and since none but the
final volume in each series ever seems to

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:21.820
require the two-part treatment, the strategy
can be seen as something of a cash grab.

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:26.640
Perhaps the most famous example is Harry Potter
and the Deathly Hallows, a novel which dedicates

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:31.020
an absurd amount of its length to characters
wandering around in the woods, and which it

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:34.930
could be argued really didn't need a pair
of two-hour films to tell its story.

00:01:34.930 --> 00:01:39.380
More recently, The Hunger Games: Mockingjay
was split into two parts, which doesn't seem

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:44.310
quite so egregious; the novel is slightly
more narratively dense, and as there are only

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:48.320
three books in the series, it's understandable
that the movies' producers would seek to give

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:50.030
audiences more to chew on.

00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:54.700
Strangely, though, in the case of Potter,
the strategy paid off - but for Hunger Games,

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:55.700
not so much.

00:01:55.700 --> 00:02:00.270
Deathly Hallows Part 2 was by far the highest-grossing
film in the series, with Part 1 coming in

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:05.100
third behind Harry Potter and the Sorcerer's
Stone; by contrast, Mockingjay Part 2 was

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:09.869
actually the series' lowest-grossing entry,
with Part 1 coming in second behind Catching

00:02:09.869 --> 00:02:10.869
Fire.

00:02:10.869 --> 00:02:15.279
Of course, Hunger Games wasn't quite the box
office force that Potter was, so the not-so-surprising

00:02:15.279 --> 00:02:19.420
takeaway here is that the greater your franchise's
cultural reach, the more likely it is that

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:23.079
audiences will shell out for two tickets to
see the conclusion of the story.

00:02:23.079 --> 00:02:27.720
With a worldwide gross of nearly $6 billion
and the two most recent entries in the main

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:32.879
series having cleared $1 billion globally,
the Fast franchise is one for which the strategy

00:02:32.879 --> 00:02:34.680
could pay off, big time.

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:38.810
Obviously, box office numbers like that tend
to prompt studios to look for ways to extend

00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:42.569
franchises for as long as possible, which
brings us to those spin-offs.

00:02:42.569 --> 00:02:47.989
Hobbs &amp; Shaw is, for the time being, the #4
all-time earner in the series, behind only

00:02:47.989 --> 00:02:52.049
Fast and Furious 6, The Fate of the Furious,
and Furious 7.

00:02:52.049 --> 00:02:57.520
Dwayne "The Rock" Johnson is a top box office
draw, his co-star Jason Statham looks ready

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:01.799
to keep kicking ass forever, and the two have
excellent onscreen chemistry; it wouldn't

00:03:01.799 --> 00:03:06.529
surprise us at all if Hobbs &amp; Shaw were to
become its own franchise, and while no sequel

00:03:06.529 --> 00:03:10.669
has yet been confirmed, the flick's three
post-credits scenes sure seemed to be setting

00:03:10.669 --> 00:03:11.669
one up.

00:03:11.669 --> 00:03:16.510
Here's what has been confirmed: According
to Entertainment Weekly, an as-yet untitled,

00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:20.299
all-female spin-off is likely to center on
Michelle Rodriguez' Letty.

00:03:20.299 --> 00:03:24.669
Diesel himself has talked up the project,
and it's currently being scripted by Guardians

00:03:24.669 --> 00:03:28.840
of the Galaxy writer Nicole Perlman, Lindsey
Beer - who's currently working on the upcoming

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:33.400
Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles reboot - and
Geneva Robertson-Dworet, who penned Captain

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:34.400
Marvel.

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:37.659
With a wealth of great characters to choose
from, the Fast universe could spin off in

00:03:37.659 --> 00:03:42.209
untold directions once the main series comes
to a close; heck, those spin-offs might even

00:03:42.209 --> 00:03:45.769
introduce new characters which could receive
spin-offs of their own in the future.

00:03:45.769 --> 00:03:50.349
As long as it keeps bringing the high-octane
action, fans will keep lining up for the latest

00:03:50.349 --> 00:03:53.569
installment - and, speaking of which, you
may want to mark your calendar.

00:03:53.569 --> 00:03:56.089
F9 comes roaring into theaters on May 22,
2020.

00:03:56.089 --> 00:03:57.109
